if statements are the primary structure used to execute a block of code only if a certain condition is met (true). They skip the code inside { } if the condition is false. Note: the condition must result in a value convertible to a boolean.

Syntax:
if (condition1)
{
  // executed if condition1 is true
}
// following statements
x = x + 1;

Example:
int x;
cin >> x;
if (x < 0)
{
  x = -x;
}
cout << x << endl;

else statements are always optional and will execute when if conditions are false. This creates a mutually exclusive (one or the other) code execution structure.

Syntax:
if (condition1)
{
  // executed if condition1 is true
}
else
{
  // executed if condition1 above is false
}
// following statements

The flowchart shows condition splitting into 'If Block Statements' (True) or 'Else Block Statements' (False), both converging into 'Following statements'. Numbered annotations (1, 2, 3) map the flowchart stages to the corresponding lines of code.

Got more than 2 cases? Use 'else if'. You can have any number of else if statements; else if is optional. Reminder: else is also optional. Curly braces { } indicate the code block associated with each if, else if, or else.

Syntax:
if (condition1)
{ // executed if condition1 is true }
else if (condition2)
{ // executed if condition2 is true but condition1 was false }
else if (condition3)
{ // executed if condition3 is true but condition1 and condition2 were false }
else
{ // executed if neither condition above is true }

What will each implementation print if 'grade' is 95?

Version 1 (independent if statements, any number can execute):
if (grade >= 90) cout << "A range";
if (grade >= 80) cout << "B range";
if (grade >= 70) cout << "C range";
if (grade >= 60) cout << "D range"; else cout << "Not gonna happen!";

Version 2 (if..else if..else chain, only one block executes):
if (grade >= 90) cout << "A range";
else if (grade >= 80) cout << "B range";
else if (grade >= 70) cout << "C range";
else if (grade >= 60) cout << "D range";
else cout << "Not gonna happen!";

Using multiple ifs when if..else if..else is appropriate is not functionally wrong but is less readable and error-prone.

Guideline: If various blocks of code are mutually exclusive, put them in an if..else if..else structure rather than many individual if statements.

BAD STYLE:
if (x < 0) { cout << "negative"; }
if (x >= 0) { cout << "positive"; }

GOOD STYLE:
if (x < 0) { cout << "negative"; }
else { cout << "positive"; }

What do we put in an if or an else statement? ANYTHING! This includes expressions and variable assignment, function calls, and even other if..else statements.

Example program:
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  double val;
  cout << "Enter a pos. #" << endl;
  cin >> val;
  if( val >= 0) {
    double res = sqrt(val);
    cout << "Result=" << res << endl;
  }
  else {
    cout << "Error!" << endl;
  }
  return 0;
}

Precedence (Order of Operations): Highest = ! (NOT), Next = && (AND), Lowest = || (OR).

Better strategy: Explicitly parenthesize everything.

Ambiguous example:
if( a>0 || !(b<5) && c==0)

Explicitly parenthesized equivalent:
if( a>0 || ((!(b<5)) && c==0))

Common Mistakes:
1. Using assignment operator (=) rather than equality check operator (==). If you accidentally use '=', it converts the assigned value to a Boolean.
2. Using multiple if statements rather than if..else or if..else if statements. Without looking at the conditions, two 'if' statements imply both could be true, while 'if..else' implies one or the other.

Example:
int main()
{
  int x, y;
  cin >> x >> y;
  // Wrong!
  if( x = 0 ) { /* some code */ }
  // Right!
  if( x == 0 ) { /* some code */ }
  // Wrong!
  if(x != y) { x = 5; }
  if(x == y) { y = 7; }
  // Right
  if(x != y) { x = 5; }
  else { y = 7; }
  return 0;
}

All conditions must be formulated as a combination of comparisons of two values at a time.

Example:
int main()
{
  int x, y;
  cin >> x >> y;
  // Wrong!
  if( 0 <= x <= 9 ) { /* some code */ }
  // Right!
  if( (0 <= x) && (x <= 9) ) { /* some code */ }
  // Wrong!
  if( x == 0 || 1 ) { /* some code */ }
  // Right!
  if( (x == 0) || (x == 1) ) { /* some code */ }
  return 0;
}

A common use of a single if statement is to deal with an exceptional case. Perform a default action before the if and then use the if to correct any exceptional cases.

General structure:
// Default action
if( /* Exceptional Case */ )
{
  // Code to apply to exceptional case
}

Example 1 (shipping fee):
bool primeMember = /* set somehow */;
double shippingFee = 7.99;
if( primeMember == true )
{
  shippingFee = 0;
}

Example 2 (manager bonus):
double salary = /* some value */;
double bonus = 0.05 * salary;
if( manager == true )
{
  bonus += 1000;
}

Connection: it's often equivalent to use 'else' for one of the cases.

Rule/Exception version:
bool primeMember = /* set somehow */;
double shippingFee = 7.99;
if( primeMember == true )
{
  shippingFee = 0;
}

Equivalent if..else version:
bool primeMember = /* set somehow */;
if( primeMember == true )
{
  shippingFee = 0;
}
else
{
  shippingFee = 7.99;
}

Name: Subcase. Description: Further divide one case into one or more subcases. Structure: Nested 'if' statements.

if( /* Condition 1 */ )
{
  // Case 1 code
  if( /* Subcondition 1a */ ) {
    // Subcase 1a code
  }
  else {
    // Subcase 1b code
  }
}
else if( /* Condition 2 */ )
{
  // Case 2 code
  if( /* Subcondition 2a */ ) {
    // Subcase 2a code
  }
}

Nested if statements form a kind of AND relationship. An if..else if chain performing the same action in multiple branches could be replaced with an OR condition (e.g., classifying as Underclassmen if freshman OR sophomore).

Structural OR example (equivalent forms):
if(level=="freshman"){cout<<"Underclassmen";} else if(level=="sophomore"){cout<<"Underclassmen";} else if(level=="junior"){cout<<"Upperclassmen";} else if(level=="senior"){cout<<"Upperclassmen";}
is equivalent to: if(level=="freshman" || level=="sophomore"){cout<<"Underclassmen";}

Structural AND example (equivalent forms):
if(category=="manager" && title=="ceo"){bonus = 0.1*salary + 1000;}
is equivalent to nested ifs: if(category=="manager"){bonus=.1*salary; if(title=="ceo"){bonus+=1000;}} else {bonus=.05*salary;}

What do we put in an if condition? ANYTHING. The compiler will interpret what is in the parentheses as a Boolean: 0 = false, Non-0 = true.

Example:
int main()
{
  int x, y, val;
  bool quit;
  cin >> x >> y >> val >> quit;
  // Uses Boolean result of comparison
  if( x > 0 ) { /* code */ }
  // Uses value of bool variable. Executes if quit == true.
  if( quit ) { /* code */ }
  // Interprets number as a bool. Executes if val is non-zero
  if( val ) { /* code */ }
  // Interprets return value as bool. Executes if the min is non-zero
  if( min(x,y) ) { /* code */ }
  return 0;
}

Precedence (Order of Operations): Highest = ! (NOT), Next = && (AND), Lowest = || (OR). Better strategy: Explicitly parenthesize everything.

Ambiguous example:
bool a, b, c;
if( a && !b || c) { /* Some code */ }

Explicitly parenthesized equivalent:
bool a, b, c;
if( (a && (!b)) || c) { /* Some code */ }

Doubles effectively represent a number in binary using a technique similar to scientific notation (e.g., +7.54*10^5). However, performing operations on doubles often leads to slight rounding errors -- one result might yield +7.541*10^5 while another yields +7.539*10^5. This makes comparison difficult, so we rarely check for exact equality. Instead, we take the difference of the two numbers, take the absolute value (abs() in <cmath>), and see if it is within a small epsilon.

Example:
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  double x, y;
  x = 1.0 / 10;
  y = 0.1;
  // Wrong!
  if( x == y ) { /* some code */ }
  // Right!
  if( abs(x-y) < 1e-6 ) { /* some code */ }
  return 0;
}
